#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128853 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "e46cfe46-965d-5c28-90ed-060ec39751a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12376" :question/problem "Given that ![](images/14/14df7999d11afbe9002e05521d71f5008f2b49250238a714fada7dcc4aeb86d2.svg) express the following integral in polar coordinates:\n\n![](images/00/009865419103717553af1a73b3de0ac958a76eb2fdc9c95467236581d3ad0e11.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n![](images/82/829d32a5563a183ecc1f646b20fba584838dffb9ce67dcc0e014d2a90bcb7add.svg)\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region ![](images/f7/f7c8d853846850d1bf4afd67f7544f3f3c3756eb0de33ee5c27985e83449997d.svg)\n\n![](images/e4/e447e688e0f3e2c0411129e8d1baf4a900008173443f5392d98af52a27eb285b.png)\n\nNotice that the region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) represents the disk of radius ![](images/f5/f5f3224583db57b9cfe323c8489ef5e5271000002b5ea253eb98fd067a858b2d.svg) centered at ![](images/37/37cb790b6a640626cb093bf7ee969ddc9aafd1eb91c86d96a5339e6df2555fca.svg) \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n![](images/df/dfe1b368d21a4745d71b5fe084246ce0a16cb6a85ad983598442d0dbde0ed591.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ![](images/ae/ae3d9424cc536ab651dc8732546bd8bfea96901ce744793ec8797c02fc446ba4.svg) in polar coordinates:\n![](images/11/11d7065eda072cefb2c9ab63e679d3a0e7103a146a37b21440f0ff2e4f393369.svg) \n\nSince ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have ![](images/3f/3f7ce8399dec23cec965849d4623642dcaf947ca8500a837ce8ef771a42b7ee3.svg)\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) and ![](images/b9/b98533dee87a7bd8ed4d593a2aabd517621d6ab96e40a5b2ab4a8ac1923ddac5.svg) First of all, ![](images/3a/3a4ef1c1b261a53f9c45d0c90f2d44b155125fe1e7b1359406830111b4846f2f.svg) can vary from ![](images/63/632079982c24b29d4de5e322230ed61f106b1715de40fdb8b6879e982a9f3955.svg) (at the origin) to ![](images/4b/4b56a88cc6e7ba7c13d83cf18ea0f759a70af4d07186f2be1c558e25589e1c7c.svg) (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/52/522df0e95e36cd67f34ffcc0ec4757c35749eab5d5137215c456c8b7e90994ae.png)\n\nSecondly, ![](images/06/06e47e2878930095abe73547809f3c9f87be3cd9b3b9069ca582970b60598840.svg) can vary between ![](images/64/64fd2741e70e8f1d2a64c58d05dc7124c6e6e94a5d7dea30124e4200e8c61e75.svg) (the negative ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis) and ![](images/b7/b76ea8adee01315895dd8c61949a5c8f28235929b53bbff59c58856e04ebd63f.svg) (the positive ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-axis):\n\n![](images/6a/6ad24b638877941296df25869f8b0ee6f9dc1e73a779a8a0da01de35d9f74f42.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates ![](images/49/4974a18e033f45691b0d3cab8bd7a16744004e4303f44184bb7045a7c64e6f8c.svg) the region can be expressed as \n![](images/9c/9cab9c4a9d446ee9fd9794a9455d46b63df1b53ce505236d7b91816cc6496890.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n![](images/5d/5d809200294cb2ddc88d35101eb6fc449902fce4ebb0b0fd9c883a32ee5c3b0c.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129016 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "d3ae6c85-14bd-57ad-b8cc-1cebb53683b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-7346" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\pi/8} \\int_0^{\\sin2\\theta} r \\, \\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{[{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the double-angle formula\n\n$$\n\\cos{2x} = 1-2\\sin^2 x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2\\theta$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin^22\\theta = \\dfrac12\\left(1-\\cos4\\theta\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we can finish our integral by integrating with respect to $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(0-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}-\\frac{2}{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32}(\\pi -2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/id #uuid "059deff9-dfa3-5c3e-88b1-194af41d3ed0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7347" :question/problem "![](images/17/1782385c55493892be0308ede1e4fbe5308ea9440e21b70cc2da7d7f01fb8dcd.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D y \\sqrt{x^2+y^2} \\: \\textrm{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\Big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "f31dd085-70fa-5eed-adf9-89fb0e6654e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7348" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x, y) = 15xy^2$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x \\geq 0,y \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/05/051cfbbf3fd37f01ff7e4442fccdc5ec4ce506e3c452b68bfa2ed5ae6e5cf6b9.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\:0\\leq \\theta \\leq  \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({2}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =32{[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32({sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{sin}^{3}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =32(1-0) \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d298631-2765-5ac2-b97f-679ad8916a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276009} {:answer/id #uuid "40abc3c2-e544-59b8-9c14-f0e4a01c790c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276041} {:answer/id #uuid "97eed52a-8abb-5db8-9604-798e79ac4216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276843} {:answer/id #uuid "9c39af7e-789b-5aed-9430-612b825a204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276878} {:answer/id #uuid "b24b07d6-5486-598f-99db-263fe3587090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}{e}^{r}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c39af7e-789b-5aed-9430-612b825a204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276878} :answer-field/id #uuid "4fde4879-96cb-5c11-ab63-d504fc117051" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ebe3923c-2578-5c4c-8b9f-d97199b48c08" :question/math-academy-id "q-161038" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa5b04b98d3796656fca912d8dc1732ea7e12bab9083f8ca543ad5fd4fdb10ff.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00aa38ea-bac0-52a0-86dc-bc6b9414c3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389121} {:answer/id #uuid "d3398719-8fe7-5303-a753-c43f72bb0596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389122} {:answer/id #uuid "b175c8ee-c27d-5255-8737-063a160f1607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{16}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389123} {:answer/id #uuid "1a083a94-f096-531d-ba65-44631b40cc71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389124} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca6e142-ca44-5238-9857-d72f2dd5f577" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(r+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00aa38ea-bac0-52a0-86dc-bc6b9414c3ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389121} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c650825-40f2-5963-96ba-a65375cf218b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a368d06f-dd38-5739-a1e1-1669cc437d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161041" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b077d20bcb79f293383a7810d1c46ad42ce78d17a8d631b01f66a9fd547f475.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $4$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1)r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+1)\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430774 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37e9bbb5-54b5-5e26-a0a0-50d7cf20ec9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430735} {:answer/id #uuid "6ef3239f-a18f-5059-bc4c-90c9f800c498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430746} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430759} {:answer/id #uuid "f3db7076-912a-5dca-b78b-50d779ed3d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430761} {:answer/id #uuid "f55622cc-0b32-52f4-8ae8-9cf843cdb023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{25}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb9c6068-17a0-54f3-9151-2a048c9f0abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430759} :answer-field/id #uuid "e75c2f8e-ad0e-567f-ad60-4f9161bdb2d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b93ec44e-b9fb-53f6-875f-5ef36dc8b48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161043" :question/problem "![](images/2d/2db1e29e5f911a45c4c48ab41c4ad6dc63eeb36a8112672ae66844e14342f4c0.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the circle shown above. What is ${∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A$ expressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $5$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 5,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{5}{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf5461b-da0e-570c-b9aa-467a2f82071d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430741} {:answer/id #uuid "5d101e08-dd8a-5f3e-96a5-138bde512f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430742} {:answer/id #uuid "681f0aba-b493-51e6-a417-42799a6f3a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430744} {:answer/id #uuid "73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430748} {:answer/id #uuid "e5117ec4-182c-58a0-848d-ff0ac8238d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73fa4118-327c-5da0-ace4-6324c8542c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430748} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4db397-2205-5620-8d4c-677784accd64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "414bd17b-1eab-5df7-bb84-eb1080abae7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161048" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (1-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-(-1-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a78120fd-44f8-51c1-b9dd-5784c7ddde90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276989} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf96883-ff35-5cf9-b8c9-ef1449820eff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277149} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cf35-e4e8-5645-988c-d9decc7eb447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277218} {:answer/id #uuid "d256a689-9115-5115-b8cc-dca87fe713d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277332} {:answer/id #uuid "f1b78b6c-d3db-508c-85cc-e0ca239ccf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3b7cf35-e4e8-5645-988c-d9decc7eb447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277218} :answer-field/id #uuid "2939712c-1519-5b89-99ce-fbfcd86f2f1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aff73867-9b1f-5562-9960-420de4061c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-161049" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\frac{1}{3}{sin}^{3}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}[(1-\\frac{1}{3})-0] \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ad79092-f016-53f7-a552-da7705b0e553" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9+4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275728} {:answer/id #uuid "7636b055-40d1-5da4-992b-6cf4130c550f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276544} {:answer/id #uuid "bed5cbac-2b75-59e8-bf61-95fb98959df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277178} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd67c0a-3dac-561a-8db6-3731e772cb7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18+2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277422} {:answer/id #uuid "f22075cf-3f26-53dc-89c2-6eefbee0d8bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bed5cbac-2b75-59e8-bf61-95fb98959df9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277178} :answer-field/id #uuid "f825344c-a3e7-5d9c-a7f6-4fa9aa047ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad9dc383-588b-5128-942d-8b56b5fb2221" :question/math-academy-id "q-161052" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=4{y}^{2}$ over the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/0a/0a1de805051eff4ea82b4eefb7bb113c724b3d4e7304704831b6294ee4e4d674.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}4(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta (81-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =81{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}{[\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}[(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =81\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278370 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a3a50e4-5411-52b3-a962-4147981c80b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276132} {:answer/id #uuid "971cd55b-3477-5ef3-9184-3dd244f49480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276833} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf4833c-b73d-57fe-b477-fadb221b09e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277159} {:answer/id #uuid "cb63beea-6aa8-51c4-97e1-f8b665be72c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a719b4-a204-5304-94dc-010ad58340ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276970} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3661af2-fdbf-5479-8627-1d09d5073cf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6beb18bc-dc57-5d1c-8540-705d9f63089f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161067" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/86/86036fd86096355b969da695f812c9bf835880edd68f7991183b70a6234e3eb4.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(2,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =4 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/9e/9ef22dcc15175664331d989bd50d9a8d60a354a0780dbaf9465caca04f28c2d9.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the positive $y$-axis):\n\n![](images/f7/f724daf8321195815c9e413aab2106c611bc9d2e9653939266fc828aebeca68c.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1091c5-d81a-565f-a281-b2fb0d00c973" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Not Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} {:answer/id #uuid "52d5e922-bccb-5fce-b300-691e090faea1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276212} {:answer/id #uuid "7df546c6-3376-55f8-94c7-e5be74bd7136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276624} {:answer/id #uuid "acefb97e-6196-52ba-a16d-9390374236cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277032} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3e9490-2677-5e30-aba6-03b5cf96c58e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b90531f-8c48-528f-be8c-e6dbd51abb0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275998} :answer-field/id #uuid "5007391b-6a4b-5cf3-b468-d5880451e5ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4206eea6-b522-5853-935f-b0d344e48a0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161070" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 16,\\,y\\le 0\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d8/d882bf70ecb7b94eee0eeeffbf63874ca8607268167f96278deb4678ec3b827a.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -4)}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16+{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/60/605d9ce1c9030d1ac83c0a320bd648b42893f222279dd2d56b111acd9957c202.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (the negative $y$-axis) and $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/f3/f37e34042338af2b1ba5f0c214ebf046eeba068b0b3238b662910a868c069ee4.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r{e}^{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c4a273a-2b19-5303-a5a5-11019a9369f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321746} {:answer/id #uuid "872c79ef-16ea-5094-9ae9-66db9b44bd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322219} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa25f5-4394-5894-a3d6-3a8ed7446150" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322320} {:answer/id #uuid "99c28fe9-f00e-52ea-be86-9376faf9d769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322369} {:answer/id #uuid "d2de48da-46c8-5206-a5b5-40a93d82b30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c4a273a-2b19-5303-a5a5-11019a9369f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321746} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c23bd6d-2dad-5455-a325-95bbab5758ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "695450ef-6209-5a8e-874a-0e67f88a5889" :question/math-academy-id "q-38076" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bccbe3973fa63908f5e264f4566e1207c5019dc1de9c36ef42481428527aef3b.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}2{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59029508-b3c4-5856-9e82-8be13d20dfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321849} {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} {:answer/id #uuid "73b5e790-d197-591e-8023-1b42b9c19647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322050} {:answer/id #uuid "84f4df8e-914d-5611-8d98-3e64db0593e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322199} {:answer/id #uuid "a8213e0d-3524-56f9-9f0b-d587a6a113bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bfe432d-69ae-5f45-8501-b2941dd6da47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321875} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8f76a21-833b-5523-ac04-4ad00b86b928" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7818e95-0cd3-5ca9-8225-4d2224300f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38133" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(\\frac{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{2}}{2}-0)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}2{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-2\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbb1193-47be-5246-a935-a3e2f7a5d9cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321372} {:answer/id #uuid "6be44563-7ff5-5aff-888b-8534ab2d8574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321988} {:answer/id #uuid "7d81d17b-e0e4-5462-aa1e-2daeda378c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322134} {:answer/id #uuid "9e663173-e8bf-5e07-9a64-eabd853d519d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322395} {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c307b8a7-c480-5cfb-a7fb-ea588c87cd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322662} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b4bbfb-a31e-55b3-9e9c-d9e99a687a4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a604ede7-8a36-5960-82df-176ca647b425" :question/math-academy-id "q-38140" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=2x+y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/b5/b5ba69ab02aa13e201b7b9113f6a36e705ad19e6412499504afc4f239459a8f3.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{3}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}((2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ))-(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430733} {:answer/id #uuid "4e7ef1ee-27c1-50f3-af3d-a2b63e2d03d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430738} {:answer/id #uuid "5b3e76f1-42b1-5f3d-8b32-cf741baa2a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430740} {:answer/id #uuid "5e28ab03-180e-5b20-af37-557e7db4d91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430743} {:answer/id #uuid "77346f94-7bc7-5551-a9ff-5850737d6c7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ea2ccdb-2b2b-5e06-a8bd-a29481af05f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430733} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ca73348-922c-58d8-8087-d4874ab8f01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c4c9c61-2471-5bbd-bac4-3d2dcc4d6268" :question/math-academy-id "q-83514" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}[{r}^{2}]_{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta }^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}({2}^{2}-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}(4-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =[4\\theta +\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-(\\pi +1) \\\\  & =\\pi -1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430770 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c68a910-d47a-5d42-8472-65a137268360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430736} {:answer/id #uuid "6f84c6cd-963a-5926-9438-431cae8fd173" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430747} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c001e9-e046-5dfd-859e-0f7553c95834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430751} {:answer/id #uuid "c1c797d0-94a3-5402-8500-48167fa5bf9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430754} {:answer/id #uuid "f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f171ab4d-9dad-5bbc-b321-f960560419b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430760} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8566ad-8855-54ed-9229-fd53a23bc1a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2077302e-eca5-5aa1-8731-3c58ab648006" :question/math-academy-id "q-83520" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=x-y$ over the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/7b/7b181b47af7d29b68d86d26b5c2b948c17ce39c01a62cbb36e874bb733865718.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x-y\\,dA & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\frac{1}{3}{[{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )({2}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129199 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38fd4660-39ca-585f-8e3a-5d8b569be77d" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Double Integral Over a Circle or Part-Circle Centered at the Origin"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c2bf0f-9ee2-5d88-8b23-545cf4448435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430734} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5313f3-0d47-59ad-80f4-3575ea706678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430750} {:answer/id #uuid "bdef2d30-346b-534b-81e2-deb246402953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430753} {:answer/id #uuid "ccbd5dd6-3cb9-5c6a-a847-fed35c7db186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{-\\sqrt{3}}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430755} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7a98739-7f64-53ee-a143-4b1433040ca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430758} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2c5fff5-18f1-537f-babe-36cf80cdf6a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bb2e441-17ac-5548-ad1a-c8ae9611922f" :question/math-academy-id "q-83954" :question/problem "![](images/a6/a60d80425c240deb1ea372d7a21c3774247c4827536ce6f409f7e8736c7c94bc.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the quarter-circle shown above. What is\n${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. In polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129198 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bc5322f-ed04-53d2-8ce6-20ec58f73a5e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Repeated Integrals Expressed in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40fcf753-0267-53ac-aa07-33aca198c3c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430737} {:answer/id #uuid "566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430739} {:answer/id #uuid "686056c3-1712-5837-8d5c-57ad9de19f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430745} {:answer/id #uuid "a84ffeca-f3ff-5817-85cf-b80c75007d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430752} {:answer/id #uuid "e43ffd51-6f41-504e-a3ea-d2e5f89becd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "566fc95d-9cf1-5eeb-ae90-c8f8a3969e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430739} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a63565-3b3e-5f4e-ac3a-4fbb44e6e16d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9231aa24-53fe-5011-80b7-3b4b62ca8c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-83966" :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we integrate with respect to $r,$ treating $\\theta $ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]_{1}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ((2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate with respect to $\\theta :$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{5\\pi /3}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi ))-(-\\frac{4}{3}{cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)-(-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2})] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} {:answer/id #uuid "115c217e-8e0a-5c5b-8302-ae521adc07f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128805} {:answer/id #uuid "232b05d7-2819-5154-96f7-710edeff9ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128806} {:answer/id #uuid "4505c771-cef0-508f-89e7-d4849ae94ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128807} {:answer/id #uuid "6325e896-5085-50b5-ab25-23a0ebf6535e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd51e610-6f03-5601-be97-90a082867f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128804} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8292c76-a1e0-5537-aaa6-9b5b73963ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "929376bc-8d45-5684-ae94-b99b8cb09d33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83968" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1a5a140c3a2d430312d6f6ceeb21d6b7282119c8ec53365553ff845d8c64d5.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where the region $D$ is the semicircle shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({sin}^{5}⁡0-{sin}^{5}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129200 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b97f75d-c784-5f3a-984d-f9a5fdf21d61" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Double Integral by Converting To Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04c8b8e0-2cc2-5fe5-a1df-e98cd1001f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351613} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4bd1d1-6340-5981-bf6a-672c766b04a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352654} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e43f8a-2b13-5ebe-b51e-312ffdb9f42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353302} {:answer/id #uuid "de54c0b4-a6ca-5c07-9dbe-5dedc4f5b648" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353496} {:answer/id #uuid "fa31fa69-bb59-5f48-98b0-b5486fce2c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa31fa69-bb59-5f48-98b0-b5486fce2c71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353757} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbf98d97-f14f-5123-b9e9-537f4bff4e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a65ae57-db8c-559b-a30a-84cd82c23ff1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83970" :question/problem "Integrate the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ over the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0$ and $y\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/e4/e4275e74a45deb22c0ed22546772e33fbd5caeb89a4036b1a2f4870e8ecf67a1.png)\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\pi \\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2[\\theta ]_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
